As suspected, `TextCommentFragment` is passing the `text` of the message to the `ZeroWidthView` component. The `text` is extracted from the `fragment` prop, which is of type `Message`. This `Message` object likely contains the full emoji art string.

So, the execution flow is as follows:

1.  A chat message containing emoji art is rendered.
2.  `TextCommentFragment` receives the message content as a `fragment`.
3.  The `text` from the `fragment` is passed to the `ZeroWidthView` component.
4.  `ZeroWidthView` calls `EmojiUtils.isFirstLetterEmoji` with the long emoji art string.
5.  The complex regex in `isFirstLetterEmoji` takes a very long time to execute, causing the app to freeze and eventually crash.

Now that I have a clear understanding of the problem, I can devise a solution. The core issue is the performance of the `isFirstLetterEmoji` function on long strings. I can optimize this by not running the regex on the entire string. Since we only care about the *first letter*, I can truncate the input string to a reasonable length before matching.

A single emoji character, even with modifiers, will not be excessively long. I'll propose a change to `isFirstLetterEmoji` to only check the first few characters of the string. A length of 10 should be more than sufficient to capture any single emoji.

Here's the proposed change to `src/libs/EmojiUtils.ts`:

```typescript
// old
function isFirstLetterEmoji(message: string): boolean {
    const trimmedMessage = Str.replaceAll(message.replace(/ /g, ''), '\n', '');
    const match = trimmedMessage.match(CONST.REGEX.EMOJIS);

    if (!match) {
        return false;
    }

    return trimmedMessage.startsWith(match[0]);
}

// new
function isFirstLetterEmoji(message: string): boolean {
    // We only need to check the first few characters of the message to see if it's an emoji.
    const messageStart = message.substring(0, 10);
    const trimmedMessage = Str.replaceAll(messageStart.replace(/ /g, ''), '\n', '');
    const match = trimmedMessage.match(CONST.REGEX.EMOJIS);

    if (!match) {
        return false;
    }

    return trimmedMessage.startsWith(match[0]);
}
```

This change will significantly reduce the input size for the regex, preventing the performance bottleneck and the subsequent crash. The rest of the application logic can remain the same.

I'll now apply this change to the `EmojiUtils.ts` file.

